# Duet firmware files that a separately published build replaces.
#
# One line per file:
#
#   <file> sha256:<checksum of the published build> <https location>
#
# The file has to be one the reprapfirmware package delivers below
# /opt/dsf/sd/firmware, because that is the name RepRapFirmware derives from
# the board type when it flashes; a build under any other name is never used.
# Only a published build is taken, checked against the pin: no local path, no
# fallback, a mismatch stops the build. See "Replacing a Duet firmware file"
# in the README, in particular why the build needs a version string of its
# own before any board is flashed with it.
#
# Example:
#   Duet3Firmware_TOOL1LC.bin sha256:0123...cdef https://github.com/Meltingplot/RepRapFirmware/releases/download/v3.7.0-rc.1+mp1/Duet3Firmware_TOOL1LC.bin
# Expansion boards: Duet3Expansion 3.7-dev at upstream 3.7.1-rc.1 (8fe8918b),
# built by us against the same library forks as the mainboard build below.
# Since 3.7.0+1.mp.5 the receivers on both sides check the length of every CAN
# message; since 3.7.1-rc.1+mp.1 both use CANlib c9120ff, whose checks no
# longer reject valid messages. The message formats stay the same. On the
# TOOL1LC our fixes keep ADC conversions from timing out while a TMC UART
# transfer runs. The printer configuration reads the hotend PT1000 through
# the TOOL1LC's second ADC (*20.temp0, the SDADC), which on the lab printer
# removed the offset of that reading. A tool board heater no longer switches
# off while it resyncs, and the reset data of the SAMC21 survives a reset right
# after it was saved.
Duet3Firmware_TOOL1LC.bin sha256:f36d7caf56da46d351124282addfd55686e96cafca569658ceaaa73721c2edf7 https://github.com/Meltingplot/Duet3Expansion/releases/download/3.7.1-rc.1%2Bmp.1/Duet3Firmware_TOOL1LC.bin
Duet3Firmware_EXP1HCL.bin sha256:1bd199593d11ccf29aa6d5d56f45b2a6d27064cdf06f20286f6299758d1c4485 https://github.com/Meltingplot/Duet3Expansion/releases/download/3.7.1-rc.1%2Bmp.1/Duet3Firmware_EXP1HCL.bin
Duet3Firmware_SZP.bin sha256:031b337b14ac23a2881590d3f518c5a3cf1881f497988aa07d766b6a363fb3eb https://github.com/Meltingplot/Duet3Expansion/releases/download/3.7.1-rc.1%2Bmp.1/Duet3Firmware_SZP.bin
# Mainboard: RepRapFirmware 3.7-dev at upstream 3.7.1-rc.1 (2092f4148), built
# by us against the Meltingplot library
# forks with a version string of its own, so DuetControlServer flashes it on
# an image update. The firmware reports its
# accelerometers under sensors.accelerometers, where DuetSoftwareFramework
# 3.7.0-rc.2 and later look for them; 3.7.0-rc.1 does not. Since 3.7.0 it
# loads the MB6HC's IAP image at 0x20458000 again, where the packaged
# Duet3_SBCiap32_MB6HC.bin is linked, and carries our fixes for macro and file
# requests over the SBC. Our CoreN2G discards the instructions the SAME70
# fetches from flash while reading the user signature, which made an MB6HC on
# 3.7.0-rc.2+1-mp.3 hard-fault in M122. On top of upstream, the main task
# carries out G-code requests from other tasks, and losing an established SBC
# connection is an emergency stop, so the mainboard resets whenever
# DuetControlServer restarts or the Pi reboots and runs config.g again once
# it reconnects. Since 3.7.0+1.mp.1 every emergency stop also turns ATX power
# off, which on the CHX 350 switches the 24 V and 48 V supply (out0 in
# e-stop.g of the printer configuration). Trigger 0 fires when its
# expression cannot be evaluated, M581.1 takes an action (U) that RRF runs
# without the SBC and a confirmation time (D), and L1 locks a trigger until
# the next restart, as trigger 0 and every trigger with an action are anyway.
# The printer configuration uses none of these trigger features yet. Before
# a single file abort and before a message box, the two requests that can
# follow a batch of file codes, the firmware writes the code replies it has
# queued, so neither overtakes the reply of a code in that batch. Since
# 3.7.0+1.mp.5 that is Duet3D's fix (f2e44bcc9) in place of ours from
# 3.7.0+1.mp.2; aborting all files, an error path, does not wait for the
# replies. The firmware no longer replies to an M99 that ends a macro file;
# DuetSoftwareFramework completes that M99 itself since 3.7.0+mp.6 and
# reports it as cancelled before. The # operator on an object model value
# that is null is an error now, where it used to return null. Since
# 3.7.0+1.mp.4 a running trigger macro no longer makes state.status busy by
# itself, as the daemon never did; its moves, a blocking message box it opens
# and heater tuning still do. The daemon.g of the printer configuration counts
# busy as activity and resets its heater idle timer on it. Since 3.7.0+1.mp.6
# the MB6HC offers capabilities of the SPI link in limits.sbcLinkCapabilities
# (phase guard, sequence guard, extended link with response codes that
# tolerate bit errors), which DuetSoftwareFramework 3.7.0+mp.9 and later
# activate per connection; without that activation, as with an older DSF, the
# link runs as before, and a connection reset ends it. After a restart it
# arms the first transfer 10 ms later, so that an exchange DuetControlServer
# started on the falling TfrRdy edge of the restart cannot stall the link.
# Since 3.7.0+1.mp.8 it also decodes the extended response codes while it is
# not connected, as after a restart, when DuetControlServer may still send
# those of its previous connection; a restart between two exchanges used to
# leave both sides one exchange apart until DuetControlServer ran out of
# retries and connected again. Since 3.7.0+1.mp.9 the firmware tags every
# packet that belongs to a code channel with the epoch of that channel, which
# DuetSoftwareFramework 3.7.0+mp.14 and later starts anew whenever it aborts
# the channel, and drops the packets of another epoch, so that the reply or
# request of aborted work no longer reaches the code that comes after it. The
# tag sits in a header field that a peer without it ignores, so with an older
# DuetSoftwareFramework the firmware writes no tag and drops nothing. It no
# longer refuses InvalidateChannel, applies a channel abort in the main task,
# ends a code it passed on to the SBC when the channel is aborted or the
# connection is lost, writes no reply of a code of an ended connection to the
# next one, and holds the packets of a channel at the start of a connection
# until it has read the first packet of the SBC. M122 names the last packet
# the epoch dropped. Since 3.7.0+1.mp.11 it carries upstream's feed hold
# (M25.1, status held, which DuetSoftwareFramework 3.7.0+mp.14 and later
# know; the printer configuration does not use it). G30 stops with an error
# when the probe does not trigger, as G29 does, instead of tapping again from
# where the axis stalled. Our fixes since mp.9 keep
# other tasks from using freed object model data, heap memory in use by
# another task or interrupts unmasked inside protected regions; FreeRTOS ends
# a critical section with the interrupt mask of its caller, and a task that
# blocks while it holds one stops in vAssertCalled, a software reset. Since
# 3.7.1-rc.1 CAN0 can be a second CAN-FD bus (M953 I0 C1), which the printer
# configuration does not enable, M952 B0 is gone, the current loop temperature
# sensor is gone, an aborted tool change macro ends the tool change, and M906
# and M917 report the configured currents; M581 K selects what a trigger does,
# next to our M581.1 actions. Our fixes since 3.7.0+1.mp.11 end the G28 or tool
# change of a macro that the SBC aborts, stop an answer longer than a transfer
# from being sent again for ever, keep the object model going when a global
# variable is too long to report, keep file and macro requests from timing out
# while the requesting task holds the object model, and stop reads and writes
# beyond packets, binary codes and the driver data of expansion boards.
Duet3Firmware_MB6HC.bin sha256:cfcae8271141640128d47d356d31aecbbe82e3e2d3bff90e7f4aad7220f693a1 https://github.com/Meltingplot/RepRapFirmware/releases/download/3.7.1-rc.1%2Bmp.1/Duet3Firmware_MB6HC.bin
